# The Myth of the Drum Keeper

In the beginning, the sky was silent. The First People lived in perfect quiet, never knowing the voice of storms.

High above the clouds lived Tama, the Drum Keeper, who possessed a great drum carved from the heartwood of the World Tree. The drum's voice could shake mountains and split stones, so the Sky Council forbade Tama from ever playing it, fearing its power would destroy the delicate new world below.

For countless seasons, Tama obeyed. But loneliness consumed her in the silent heights. She longed to make music, to create sound that matched the beauty she saw in the world below—the dancing rivers, the swaying forests, the rushing winds.

One day, Tama could bear it no longer. She struck her drum once, tentatively. The sound rolled across the heavens—deep, resonant, magnificent. She struck it again, and again, creating rhythms that made her spirit soar.

The Sky Council was furious and banished Tama to wander the clouds forever. But they could not take her drum. To this day, when Tama's loneliness grows too great, she plays her drum, and we hear her music as thunder—the sound of beauty that cannot be contained, even by divine decree.

