# The Weaver's Tears

Long ago, when the world was newly formed, there lived a celestial weaver named Stellara who dwelled beyond the clouds. Her task was to weave the fabric of night, a great tapestry that would blanket the earth when the sun retreated.

Stellara worked tirelessly, threading darkness across the sky. But she grew lonely in her solitude, and as she wove, tears fell from her eyes. Each tear was made of pure light, and where they touched her dark tapestry, they burned small holes, shining through with brilliant radiance.

The first humans gazed up and saw these points of light, calling them stars. They wondered at their beauty, telling stories and finding patterns in them. Their joy and wonder floated up to Stellara, warming her lonely heart.

Now content, Stellara stopped crying, but the stars remained. To honor the humans who had comforted her, she decided to make the stars dance. Each night, she gently shakes her tapestry, causing the stars to shimmer and twinkle, reminding us that even in darkness, beauty can emerge from sorrow.

And so the stars continue to flicker, eternal reminders of the weaver's tears and the connection between earth and sky.

